\paragraph{The Failure of Standard BNNs.}
As summarized in Table~\ref{tab:bo_results} and illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:bo_showdown}, Standard BNN suffers catastrophic void collapse on Levy 2D. Initialized around $[-4.0, -4.0]$, the MC-Dropout variance collapses to zero outside the local cluster. Consequently, the LCB acquisition bonus vanishes ($\beta \sigma(x) \to 0$), confining all 35 sequential queries to the initialization corner and terminating with a dismal regret of \textbf{1.0108}.

\paragraph{Single-Network DP-TS Superiority.}
In stark contrast, Single-Network DP-TS draws fresh random measures $\mathcal{P}_t$ with non-vanishing void dispersion (Theorem~\ref{thm:void_dispersion}). Each freshly trained single network draws a distinct global functional hypothesis, undercutting the local cluster and discovering the global minimum at $(1, 1)$ with final regret \textbf{0.0135}---a \textbf{75$\times$ reduction in error} over Standard BNN, while outperforming even exact GP-BO ($0.0342$). On Ackley 2D, DP-TS reaches the exact global optimum (\textbf{0.0000}), completely resolving the landscape.

\subsection{High-Dimensional Continuous Control: 32D Policy Optimization}
To demonstrate scalability to complex nonlinear control tasks where Gaussian Processes struggle, we deploy DP-TS to \textbf{32-dimensional continuous policy optimization} on the OpenAI Gym \texttt{Pendulum-v1} environment.

\paragraph{Problem Formulation.}
The policy is parameterized as a compact neural controller with weights $W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 8}$ and $W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{8 \times 1}$, defining a 32-dimensional continuous optimization problem: $\mathbf{x} = \mathrm{vec}(W_1, W_2) \in [-2.0, 2.0]^{32}$. The objective $f(\mathbf{x}) = -\mathbb{E}[\sum_t r_t]$ evaluates the cumulative return over randomized initial conditions (lower regret is better; near-optimal swing-up is $\approx 150$, uncoordinated failure is $\approx 1600$).

