\subsection{Computational Scalability and Runtime}
Across all benchmarks (Table~\ref{tab:bo_results} and Table~\ref{tab:high_dim_results}), Single-Network DP-TS delivers decisive practical advantages:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{$5\times$ Speedup over MC-Dropout}: Standard BNN consumes $104.2$~ms/step across $S=20$ stochastic passes. DP-TS evaluates candidates with a single deterministic forward pass, dropping latency to \textbf{21.6 ms}.
    \item \textbf{$2.2\times$ Speedup over Multi-Head Ensembles}: Eliminating $K=4$ heads cuts step time from $47.7$~ms down to $21.6$~ms.
    \item \textbf{Minimal Memory Footprint}: DP-TS stores only a single network ($<15$~MB VRAM vs $>60$~MB for ensembles), with zero dense $\mathcal{O}(t^2)$ Gram matrix storage.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this work, we presented Data-Space Dirichlet Process Bayesian Neural Networks (DP-BNNs), resolving the fundamental pathology of epistemic uncertainty collapse in the data void. Rather than placing intractable priors over parameter weights, DP-BNN places a Dirichlet Process prior directly over the unknown data-generating distribution. We proved that because the Dirichlet Process posterior is an infinite-dimensional distribution over probability distributions, optimizing a \textbf{single deterministic neural network} on a freshly sampled random distribution directly generates an exact functional draw from the posterior function distribution. This enables pure, hyperparameter-free Non-Parametric Thompson Sampling (DP-TS) without ensemble or multi-head overhead. 

Theoretically, we proved that DP-TS guarantees non-vanishing epistemic void dispersion and establishes global optimization consistency with vanishing simple regret $\lim_{T \to \infty} S_T = 0$. Empirically, Single-Network DP-TS systematically overcomes deceptive local traps where conventional BNNs fail (achieving up to $75\times$ error reduction on multimodal benchmarks) and scales effectively to high-dimensional control ($d=32$) and robotics ($d=60$), delivering state-of-the-art solution quality with a rapid 21.6~ms step latency.

